title: automaton_busy_beaver
---
<<present_speaker "npc_busy_beaver">>
<<if verdict("world.flag_set", "automaton_busy_beaver_met") == "satisfied">>
    Busy Beaver: Block. Block. Block. Don't count them, you'll only fall behind.
<<elseif verdict("world.flag_set", "automaton_busy_beaver_met") == "not_satisfied">>
    <<command "world.set_flag" "automaton_busy_beaver_met" true>>
    Busy Beaver: Mind the block. That one's load-bearing. They're all load-bearing.
<<else>>
    <<stop>>
<<endif>>
-> What are you building?
    Busy Beaver: Stairs. Then whatever the stairs are for. Then more stairs.
    Busy Beaver: The chest on top is for whoever gets there first. I keep not getting there. I keep adding a step.
-> Aren't you in Math Town?
    Busy Beaver: Oh, the gate cradle! Did they ever get the replacement bracket?
    Busy Beaver: Here I've got five states and a tape. I'm trying to run for as many steps as I can and still halt.
-> When will you stop?
    Busy Beaver: For five states, the record is 47,176,870 steps. Turns out proving that nobody could halt later was a much longer job.
    Busy Beaver: For six, nobody knows. I like those odds.
-> ‹go›
===
title: automaton_moore
---
<<present_speaker "npc_automaton_neighbour_moore">>
<<if verdict("world.flag_set", "automaton_moore_met") == "satisfied">>
    Neighbour Moore: Still here. So is the block. So are you, for now.
<<elseif verdict("world.flag_set", "automaton_moore_met") == "not_satisfied">>
    <<command "world.set_flag" "automaton_moore_met" true>>
    Neighbour Moore: Evening. You're standing in my neighbourhood. That's fine. Everybody is in somebody's.
<<else>>
    <<stop>>
<<endif>>
<<jump automaton_moore__topics>>
===

title: automaton_moore__topics
---
-> What is this place?
    Neighbour Moore: The Life field. Every cell looks at its eight neighbours and decides.
    Neighbour Moore: Two or three living ones around you, and you stay. Exactly three around an empty cell, and something's born there. Any other count, and you're gone by the next generation.
    <<jump automaton_moore__topics>>
-> What's that square?
    Neighbour Moore: The block. Four cells, each with three live neighbours. It hasn't changed since the first generation.
    Neighbour Moore: I moved in beside it after a glider went straight through my old neighbourhood.
    <<jump automaton_moore__topics>>
-> How do I get across?
    Neighbour Moore: Ride a glider. Five cells that keep changing shape and then returning to the same shape a little farther along.
    Neighbour Moore: Every four generations it's moved one cell on the diagonal. Stand on it and you move too.
    Neighbour Moore: And keep clear of the spaceships. The next one should pass in a moment. Wait here until it's gone.
    <<jump automaton_moore__topics>>
-> ‹go›
===
title: automaton_head
---
<<present_speaker "npc_automaton_head">>
<<if verdict("world.flag_set", "automaton_head_met") == "satisfied">>
    The Head: Read. Write. Move. Change state. Where were we? Step 47,176,870. Halted.
<<elseif verdict("world.flag_set", "automaton_head_met") == "not_satisfied">>
    <<command "world.set_flag" "automaton_head_met" true>>
    The Head: Halted! Finally halted. Do you know how long I've wanted to say that?
<<else>>
    <<stop>>
<<endif>>
<<jump automaton_head__topics>>
===

title: automaton_head__topics
---
-> Who are you?
    The Head: The head. The part of the machine that does anything. The tape remembers, the table decides, and I go back and forth.
    The Head: I read the cell under me, write a one or a zero, move one cell left or right, and change state. That is the whole of my education.
    <<jump automaton_head__topics>>
-> What is the table on the wall?
    The Head: Five states, A to E. Two entries each: what to do on a zero, what to do on a one. Ten rules.
    The Head: Ten rules, and it runs for 47,176,870 steps, and leaves 4,098 ones on the tape. Then the rule for E on a zero says: halt.
    <<jump automaton_head__topics>>
-> How do you know it stops?
    The Head: The five-state running-time record was proved in 2024. They had to rule out every machine that might halt later.
    The Head: Every machine of five states under these rules, starting on a blank tape, that ever stops has stopped by then. I'm the one that took the longest.
    <<jump automaton_head__topics>>
-> ‹go›
===
title: automaton_pca
---
<<present_speaker "perfect_cellular_automaton">>
<<if verdict("world.flag_set", "automaton_pca_met") == "satisfied">>
    Perfect Cell-ular Automaton: Oh, excellent. I was beginning to think nobody would interrupt my afternoon.
<<else>>
    <<command "world.set_flag" "automaton_pca_met" true>>
    Perfect Cell-ular Automaton: Welcome to my arena. I built it right on the Life field. Have a look at the audience. The formation on the left is particularly lovely.
    Perfect Cell-ular Automaton: I'm the Perfect Cellular Automaton. Perfect will do.
<<endif>>
<<jump automaton_pca__ring>>
===
title: automaton_pca__ring
---
-> What is the failure log?
    Perfect Cell-ular Automaton: My guest book. I asked for a column in which to record my losses. The engraver suggested leaving it off.
    <<jump automaton_pca__ring>>
-> What's on the big screen?
    Perfect Cell-ular Automaton: A Gosper glider gun, 1970. One glider every thirty generations, indefinitely. Elegant, isn't it?
    Perfect Cell-ular Automaton: I've always found thirty generations a rather leisurely pace.
    <<jump automaton_pca__ring>>
-> Who is that on the gallery?
    Perfect Cell-ular Automaton: The Imperfect Cellular Automaton. Charming creature. I offered to show him how to dodge a glider. He dodged the demonstration.
    <<jump automaton_pca__ring>>
-> Let's fight.
    Perfect Cell-ular Automaton: Splendid! I do hope you have something I haven't seen.
    <<music "glider_gun">>
    <<challenge>>
-> ‹go›
    Perfect Cell-ular Automaton: Very well. I shall have to amuse myself.
===
title: automaton_imperfect
---
<<present_speaker "imperfect_cellular_automaton">>
<<if verdict("world.flag_set", "automaton_imperfect_met") == "satisfied">>
    Imperfect Cellular Automaton: Still not looking at the log? Good. Neither am I.
<<elseif verdict("world.flag_set", "automaton_imperfect_met") == "not_satisfied">>
    <<command "world.set_flag" "automaton_imperfect_met" true>>
    Imperfect Cellular Automaton: Don't read the log. The third row. And the fifth. Just don't read the log.
<<else>>
    <<stop>>
<<endif>>
-> You lost to Perfect?
    Imperfect Cellular Automaton: I lost to myself. Perfect just stood there. One of my cells was late, its neighbours copied it, and eight generations later I was a different pattern.
    Imperfect Cellular Automaton: One cell per generation. I could watch it coming across the row and I still couldn't stop it.
-> Why do you stay?
    Imperfect Cellular Automaton: Somebody has to cheer for the challengers. The stands won't. They're all on the rule's side.
-> ‹go›
===
